SUMMARY:Inprint Ayad Akhtar Reading
DTSTAMP:20260724T105421Z
DTSTART:20261005T193000Z
DESCRIPTION:General admission tickets will go on sale on Tuesday\, Septembe
	r 15\, 2026 at 12 pm. \nAyad Akhtar will read from his new novel The Radia
	nce\, followed by an on-stage conversation led by a local author/journalis
	t. The evening will conclude with a book sale and signing. The event is pr
	esented as part of the 2026/2027 Inprint Margarett Root Brown Reading Seri
	es.  \nPulitzer Prize winner Ayad Akhtar’s new novel The Radiance is “simp
	ly put\, a work of genius… Akhtar explores the nature of memory and of the
	 soul\, while the unfolding tale builds tension and anticipation like a wh
	odunit” (Abraham Verghese). It has been named one of the New York Time‘s “
	Novels Everyone Will Be Talking About in 2026.” A mysterious accident alon
	g a country road sparks an awakening and an investigation in Akhtar’s most
	 daring work yet—a visionary novel of spiritual transformation in an age o
	f fracture. \nAkhtar’s work as a novelist and playwright has been publishe
	d and performed in over two dozen languages. He is the winner of the Pulit
	zer Prize for Drama for his play Disgraced\, which was also nominated for 
	the Tony Award for Best Play. His other Broadway plays include McNeal\, Ju
	nk\, and The Who & The What. Akhtar is also the author of the novels Ameri
	can Dervish\, a Kirkus Reviews Best Book of the Year\, and Homeland Elegie
	s\, called “a tour de force” by The Washington Post. In 2021\, he was name
	d the New York State Author by the New York State Writers Institute\, succ
	eeding Colson Whitehead. \n\n\nhttps://inprinthouston.org/event/inprint-ay
	ad-akhtar-reading/\n\nFrom Inprint Houston
URL:https://inprinthouston.org/event/inprint-ayad-akhtar-reading/
LOCATION:Alley Theatre\, 615 Texas Avenue\, Houston\, TX\, 77002
X-CALRIPPER-SOURCE:inprint-houston
CATEGORIES:Inprint Houston
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
DURATION:PT2H
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
IMAGE;VALUE=URI;DISPLAY=BADGE;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://inprinthouston.org
 /wp-content/uploads/2026/07/Hernan-Diaz-c-Pascal-Perich_for-website.jpg
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://inprinthouston.org/wp-content/uploads/202
 6/07/Hernan-Diaz-c-Pascal-Perich_for-website.jpg
UID:c0GDfaUeceRo5wANjalh6
SUMMARY:Inprint Hernan Diaz Reading
DTSTAMP:20260724T105421Z
DTSTART:20261012T193000Z
DESCRIPTION:General admission tickets will go on sale on Monday\, September
	 21\, 2026 at 12 pm. \nHernan Diaz will read from his new novel Ply\, foll
	owed by an on-stage conversation led by a local author/journalist. The eve
	ning will conclude with a book sale and signing. The event is presented as
	 part of the 2026/2027 Inprint Margarett Root Brown Reading Series.  \nDes
	cribed by Lauren Groff as a “narrative genius\,” Hernan Diaz is the Pulitz
	er Prize-winning and bestselling author of the novel Trust\, named one of 
	the New York Times’s Best 100 Books of the 21st Century and currently bein
	g developed as a limited series for HBO. His literary work is celebrated f
	or its genre-bending reinvention of American mythology and its complex exp
	loration of wealth\, capitalism\, and isolation. His first novel\, In the 
	Distance\, was a finalist for the Pulitzer Prize and the PEN/Faulkner Awar
	d. His work has been translated into 37 languages. \nAfter rewriting Ameri
	ca’s past in his two previous novels\, from 1920s Wall Street to the 19th 
	century western frontier\, Diaz turns to the future with his new novel Ply
	. A combination of Dickensian odyssey\, family drama\, and scientific thri
	ller\, Ply examines the place of technology in the American imagination. C
	enturies from now\, the state has been dismantled\, and humans are reinven
	ting social bonds and learning new ways to coexist with nature. Following 
	a childhood defined by loss\, survival\, and found family\, an orphan grow
	s up to become a “pincher\,” someone who steals electricity from the grid 
	to sell it on the black market. “Diaz brilliantly envisions how love\, tru
	st\, and loyalty can remain when freedom and justice have collapsed and ma
	kes every page exhilarating and meaningful” (Library Journal\, starred rev
	iew). \n\n\nhttps://inprinthouston.org/event/inprint-hernan-diaz-reading/\
	n\nFrom Inprint Houston
URL:https://inprinthouston.org/event/inprint-hernan-diaz-reading/
LOCATION:Stude Concert Hall\, Rice University\, Entrance #18 & #20\, 6100 M
	ain St.\, Houston\, TX\, 77005\, United States
X-CALRIPPER-SOURCE:inprint-houston
CATEGORIES:Inprint Houston
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
DURATION:PT2H
END:VEVENT
